 Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://Carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-25","committee":"Governance & Audit Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Moved","webcast":true,"url":"https://Carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Carmarthenshire's Council Fund overspend reaches \u00a31.76m, covered by reserve draw","lead":"Carmarthenshire Council spent \u00a31.755m more than its original budget in the year to 31 March 2026, and covered the shortfall by drawing from its general reserves. The Council Fund \u2014 the main account for day-to-day running costs \u2014 finished the year with General Balances of \u00a315.497m. The council's Governance & Audit Committee received the outturn figures on 28 September. The overspend required a transfer from the reserve cushion the council holds for unexpected costs. Councils must hold sufficient reserves to manage in-year pressures and to smooth funding across years; the remaining balance provides the buffer for future risk.","iso":"2026-09-28","paper_title":"Summary","paper_url":"https://carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s111018/111018.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Carmarthenshire's day-to-day spending exceeded budget by \u00a31.755m, covered by reserves","lead":"Carmarthenshire's day-to-day running costs for the year exceeded the original budget by \u00a31.755m, requiring the council to transfer money from its General Balances \u2014 money set aside for unexpected needs. The overspend was covered entirely from reserves, leaving the Council Fund General Balances at \u00a315.497m at the end of the year. The council's own statement of accounts confirmed that overall expenditure on day-to-day spending (revenue expenditure) ran over the budgeted figure, and the shortfall was made good through the planned transfer.","iso":"2026-09-28","paper_title":"Summary","paper_url":"https://carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s111018/111018.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Dyfed Pension Fund cleared by auditors as investment income falls short by \u00a36m","lead":"Audit Wales has completed its 2025\u201326 audit of the Dyfed Pension Fund with no issues raised, giving the fund a clean financial bill of health. However, the fund is forecast to end the year \u00a32.9m in deficit, driven chiefly by investment income falling \u00a36m short of the amount budgeted. The fund was counting on that income to balance its books after employer contributions were cut following the 2025 actuarial valuation. The shortfall reflects lower distributions from fund managers and interest on cash holdings than anticipated. The fund has responded by building cash reserves to \u00a318.5m \u2014 higher than historically held \u2014 described as a prudent measure given the structural changes to contributions.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"10.1a WPP JGC update for pension committees 15 July 2026","paper_url":"https://carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110699/110699.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-28","committee":"Governance & Audit Committee","paper_title":"Summary","paper_url":"https://carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s111018/111018.pdf","headline":"Council Fund spending exceeded budget by \u00a31.755m, requiring a transfer from reserves.","detail":"The Authority's Council Fund net expenditure for the year was over the original budget. The council transferred \u00a31.755m from its General Balances to cover the overspend. At year-end, Council Fund General Balances stood at \u00a315.497m.","category":"overspend","matters":"A lender assessing the council's financial management and resilience needs to know whether it can spend within budget. An overspend requiring reserve depletion indicates expenditure pressures that exceeded the council's planned resources.","figures":"\u00a31.755m transfer from balances; Council Fund General Balances of \u00a315.497m at year-end","quote":"Overall the Authority's Council Fund net expenditure for the year was over the original budget, resulting in a transfer of \u00a31.755m from balances on the Council Fund","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Dyfed Pension Fund Committee","paper_title":"Summary","paper_url":"https://carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110676/110676.pdf","headline":"Fund cash balances increased to \u00a318.5m, higher than previously held, in response to structural changes","detail":"Cash holdings increased from \u00a314.3m in April to \u00a318.5m in June 2026. The fund has deliberately built higher cash reserves than historically held as a prudent measure. Cash is held by Carmarthenshire County Council and invested in the council's investment portfolio earning interest.","category":"reserves","matters":"Higher liquidity buffers indicate active risk management in response to structural funding changes, but also represent capital reserves deployed defensively rather than in fund investments.","figures":"\u00a318.5m cash as at 30 June; April \u00a314.3m; May \u00a315.7m; June \u00a318.5m","quote":"The cash balances held are higher than the Fund has held previously. Due to the reduced employer contributions, it has felt that holding higher cash balances is prudent.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Dyfed Pension Fund Committee","paper_title":"Summary","paper_url":"https://carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110676/110676.pdf","headline":"Investment income \u00a36m below budget, creating the funding gap","detail":"Investment income is forecast at \u00a36m less than the amount budgeted. The budget was structured as zero-based, meaning investment income was the balancing variable. The shortfall directly creates the \u00a32.9m overall deficit.","category":"income","matters":"The fund depends on investment performance to balance its budget, creating direct exposure to market conditions that councils or members must absorb.","figures":"\u00a36m investment income shortfall","quote":"Investment income is forecast to be \u00a36m less than budget. In setting the budget investment income demonstrates the amount required to create a zero-based budget (where income and expenditure are equal).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Dyfed Pension Fund Committee","paper_title":"Summary","paper_url":"https://carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110676/110676.pdf","headline":"Employer contributions reduced following 2025 actuarial valuation, increasing fund reliance on investment income","detail":"The 2025 actuarial valuation resulted in reduced employer contributions to the fund. This structural change has increased the fund's requirement for investment income to maintain funding levels. The fund generates investment income with a significant portion reinvested.","category":"income","matters":"This represents a material change in the council's annual pension funding obligations and alters the sustainability model of the pension fund.","figures":"Specific contribution reduction amount not stated; impact evident in \u00a32.9m deficit position","quote":"Due to a reduction in employer contributions following the 2025 Actuarial Valuation the requirement for investment income has increased.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Dyfed Pension Fund Committee","paper_title":"Summary","paper_url":"https://carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110676/110676.pdf","headline":"Fund expenditure forecast to exceed income by \u00a32.9m","detail":"Total expenditure is projected at \u00a3152.8m and total income at \u00a3149.9m. While the fund is forecast to underspend by \u00a32.1m (mainly through lower benefits and management expenses), income falls \u00a35m short of budget, primarily due to investment income being \u00a36m below the amount budgeted to achieve fund balance.","category":"overspend","matters":"Pension fund deficits directly impact the council's financial position and may require increased contributions or reserve drawdowns to meet pension payment obligations.","figures":"\u00a32.9m deficit; \u00a3152.8m total expenditure; \u00a3149.9m total income; \u00a32.1m expenditure underspend; \u00a35m income shortfall","quote":"The position as at 30 June 2026 is that expenditure is forecast to exceed income by \u00a32.9m.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-28"},"name":"Carmarthenshire","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-28","since_iso":"2026-08-29","distress":false,"headline":"Council Fund spending exceeded budget by \u00a31.755m, requiring a transfer from reserves.","lead":"A lender assessing the council's financial management and resilience needs to know whether it can spend within budget. An overspend requiring reserve depletion indicates expenditure pressures that exceeded the council's planned resources.","also":["82 other findings in this window","27 papers caught"],"watch":"Cabinet Member for Deputy Leader and Regeneration, Leisure, Culture and Tourism on 2026-09-28","n_findings":83,"n_documents":27,"top_paper_title":"Summary","top_paper_url":"https://carmarthenshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s111018/111018.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":133,"news_drafts_total":3}}